<item>
            <title>[LU-20681] guard ll_splice_alias() against re-hashing a hashed dentry</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20681</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;A client hung for 26h with 75 CPUs spinning in __d_lookup() on a dcache bucket corrupted into a self-loop (d_hash.next == &amp;amp;self); RCU then stalls and wedges umount/lsof, forcing a reboot.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Such a self-loop can only arise one way: hashing a dentry that is already the head of its bucket (__d_rehash does n-&amp;gt;next = bucket-&amp;gt;first while bucket-&amp;gt;first == &amp;amp;n). Every dcache inserter in the kernel guards against this (d_splice_alias BUG_ONs, __d_move drops before rehashing), and a full drgn census (2.3M dentries, 33M buckets) found exactly one self-loop and no other damage. The sole inserter missing a d_unhashed() check is d_add() in&lt;br/&gt;
ll_splice_alias() (llite/namei.c): the comment above it still states the invariant, but the check was removed by &lt;a href=&quot;https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-8371&quot; title=&quot;Optimize open of known negative dentry&quot; class=&quot;issue-link&quot; data-issue-key=&quot;LU-8371&quot;&gt;&lt;del&gt;LU-8371&lt;/del&gt;&lt;/a&gt; (2016), making it an unstated precondition on every caller. No other path can create this corruption.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&#160;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class=&quot;code panel&quot; style=&quot;border-width: 1px;&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;codeContent panelContent&quot;&gt;
&lt;pre class=&quot;code-java&quot;&gt;
crash&amp;gt; struct dentry.d_hash 0xff38e06f48239bc0
  d_hash = {
    next = 0xff38e06f48239bc8, &lt;span class=&quot;code-comment&quot;&gt;// The next is self...
&lt;/span&gt;    pprev = 0x0
  },
crash&amp;gt; struct dentry.d_hash -x
struct dentry {
   [0x8] struct hlist_bl_node d_hash;
} &lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description>
